pub const TASKS_LIST: &str = "List tasks with filters (project, status, priority, estimate, assignee, due date, tags, epic, scope, blocked_by, blocks, text query, title, title_exact, source_file, plan, has_plan), sorting and pagination. Use q to locate a task by id, title, description, the id of its epic or a partial tag (e.g. q='LOG-4', q='26', q='est:'). Returns: {items, total} - items is the list of matching tasks, total is the count of all matching tasks before limit/offset. limit defaults to 100 (max 1000); use offset and total to paginate. Use fields to limit returned columns; the field names it accepts are listed in the description of the fields parameter, unknown names are ignored, and id, project_id and number always stay in the result. By default each task carries short_description (first line, ~120 chars) instead of the full description; set include_description to get the full text, in which case short_description is omitted. include_project attaches project names, include_scope attaches scope names, and neither filters; project_archived filters by project archival; include_deleted also returns soft-deleted tasks (hidden by default). Task id is the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' identifier (e.g. 'LOG-82'); number is the per-project sequence number (e.g. 82), unique only inside its project. project_id accepts one id or a list of ids; exclude_project_id drops the given projects (use it for 'every project except X'). The epic filter accepts a full epic id (e.g. 'LOG-5') or a list of them; scope_id likewise takes one scope id or a list, and null matches tasks without that link. has_epic and has_scope filter by the presence of an epic or a scope. plan_id accepts an id from plans_list and matches tasks in that plan; has_plan filters by the presence of any plan; sort=plan_position orders by item position in that plan and requires plan_id. plan_ids on a task is read-only: use plans_items_add/remove to change membership. The estimate filter takes one size or a list of them ('S', 'M', 'L'); tasks without an estimate are not matched. The assignee filter takes one name or a list of them and matches case-insensitively; tasks without an assignee are not matched. The due_before and due_after filters take a date (YYYY-MM-DD) and select the tasks due before it or on and after it; tasks without a due date are not matched, so the two together give a due window. sort accepts updated_at, created_at, closed_at, priority, number and title. The blocked_by filter returns tasks whose blocked_by list contains the given task, the blocks filter returns tasks whose blocks list contains the given task. The source_file filter matches the source file path of a task partially and case-insensitively, so a fragment such as 'backlog' selects every task imported from that file. Every task carries blocked_by (tasks that block me) and blocks (tasks I block) as id lists.";

pub const TASKS_GET: &str = "Get a single task by task_id. The task_id must use the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form (e.g. 'LOG-82'); a bare number is rejected because it is unique only inside a project. Returns: the task itself - {id, project_id, number, title, description, status, priority, is_epic, epic_id, scope_id, source_file, tags, estimate, assignee, due_at, attachments_count, created_at, updated_at, closed_at, closed_reason, blocked_reason, deleted_at, project_name, scope_name, short_description, blocked_by, blocks, relates_to, duplicates, duplicated_by, children_counts, plan_ids}. For a task with is_epic=true the result also carries children_counts: the number of its child tasks per status (backlog, open, in-progress, blocked, done, cancelled) plus their total.";

pub const TASKS_CREATE: &str = "Create a task. Requires project_id and title; description, status, priority, estimate, assignee, due_at, blocked_reason, epic, scope, tags are optional. description is Markdown and formatting is rendered: a short log or config in a fenced block is welcome, source code belongs in the repository. status defaults to 'backlog', where the task waits for a later session, even if the work looks small. Pass 'open' only when you start on it right away, in this same session; 'later' from the user always means backlog. estimate is the size of the task: 'S', 'M' or 'L'. assignee is the person responsible for the task, due_at is its due date (YYYY-MM-DD). blocked_reason is one line of free text explaining a block outside the tracker and is only allowed when status is 'blocked'. epic_id accepts a full epic id (e.g. 'LOG-5') of the same project. scope is optional: pass the scope name (e.g. 'site') or its id as scope_id; a task with neither sits outside the project's scopes, and scopes_create {project_id, name} adds a scope. Tags are optional too, but they come from a closed dictionary: an unknown tag is rejected with the list of existing ones, and tags_create {name} adds a genuinely new tag. source_file names the document or file the task came from; it is provenance only, it is never verified and several tasks may share it. Returns: the created task - {id, project_id, number, title, description, status, priority, is_epic, epic_id, scope_id, source_file, tags, estimate, assignee, due_at, attachments_count, created_at, updated_at, closed_at, closed_reason, blocked_reason, deleted_at, project_name, scope_name, short_description, blocked_by, blocks, relates_to, duplicates, duplicated_by, children_counts} with a warnings array.";

pub const TASKS_UPDATE: &str = "Update fields of an existing task by task_id. Pass the fields to change next to task_id (e.g. {task_id: 'LOG-87', status: 'in-progress'}); a nested patch object {task_id, patch: {status}} is accepted too. description is Markdown and formatting is rendered: a short log or config in a fenced block is welcome, source code belongs in the repository. The task_id must use the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form (e.g. 'LOG-82'). epic_id accepts a full epic id (e.g. 'LOG-5') of the task's current project, scope takes the name of a scope of that project (case-insensitive; scope_id takes the id) and scope: null clears it, estimate takes the size of the task ('S', 'M' or 'L') or null to clear it, assignee takes the name of the responsible person or null to clear it, due_at takes the due date (YYYY-MM-DD) or null to clear it, and blocked_reason is one line of free text explaining a block outside the tracker, allowed only on a blocked task; leaving status 'blocked' clears it. The call must name at least one field with a value: no field, a field set to undefined, an unknown field or an epic id that does not exist is an error, the task is left untouched. Returns: the updated task - {id, project_id, number, title, description, status, priority, is_epic, epic_id, scope_id, source_file, tags, estimate, assignee, due_at, attachments_count, created_at, updated_at, closed_at, closed_reason, blocked_reason, deleted_at, project_name, scope_name, short_description, blocked_by, blocks, relates_to, duplicates, duplicated_by, children_counts} with a changes array and a warnings array, which is not empty when the task is blocked with neither a blocker task nor a blocked_reason, or when an epic is closed while some of its subtasks are still open. Closing the last open subtask of an epic closes the epic itself with reason 'fixed' and returns it in closed_epic; reopening a subtask of an epic that was closed automatically reopens the epic in open and returns it in reopened_epic. An unknown tag is rejected: tags are a closed dictionary, see tags_list and tags_create.";

pub const TASKS_CLOSE: &str = "Close a task by task_id with a reason: 'fixed' \u{2192} done, 'wontfix' | 'obsolete' | 'duplicate' | 'not-reproduced' \u{2192} cancelled. 'not-reproduced' marks a report that could not be reproduced. The task_id must use the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form (e.g. 'LOG-82'). Closing a task that blocks others releases them from status 'blocked' - every released task returns to the status it had before it was blocked ('open' when that status is unknown), and those tasks are listed in released. The blocking links stay in place as history, so do not remove them afterwards. Returns: the closed task - {id, project_id, number, title, description, status, priority, is_epic, epic_id, scope_id, source_file, tags, estimate, assignee, due_at, attachments_count, created_at, updated_at, closed_at, closed_reason, blocked_reason, deleted_at, project_name, scope_name, short_description, blocked_by, blocks, relates_to, duplicates, duplicated_by, children_counts} with a changes array, a released array and a warnings array, which is not empty when an epic is closed while some of its subtasks are still open. Closing the last open subtask of an epic closes the epic itself with reason 'fixed' and returns it in closed_epic.";

pub const TASKS_CREATE_MANY: &str = "Create several tasks in one call. Each item is created independently and the response reports ok/error per item; a failing item does not cancel the others. Items are created in the given order, so task numbers are allocated sequentially. Each item needs its own project_id and title and accepts the same fields as tasks_create, including epic_id as a full epic id of the item's project. Returns: {items, succeeded, failed} - every item is {index, id, ok, task, changes, released, warnings} when it worked and {index, id, ok:false, error:{kind, message}} when it failed, where index is the position in the request list and id is the id of the created task (null for a failed item). changes is empty for a created task, released is empty when nothing was released. Every item carries its own warnings array. Tags are a closed dictionary here as well: an unknown tag fails that item and leaves the others untouched.";

pub const TASKS_UPDATE_MANY: &str = "Update several tasks in one call. Each item is applied independently and the response reports ok/error per item; a failing item does not cancel the others. Every item carries task_id plus the fields to change next to it (e.g. {items: [{task_id: 'LOG-87', status: 'done'}]}); a nested patch object {task_id, patch: {...}} is accepted too. Every task_id must use the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form. Every item must name at least one field with a value: no field, a field set to undefined, an unknown field or an epic id that does not exist is an error for that item, the task is left untouched and the item reports ok:false. Returns: {items, succeeded, failed} - every item is {index, id, ok, task, changes, released, warnings} when it worked and {index, id, ok:false, error:{kind, message}} when it failed, where index is the position in the request list and id is the id of the task. released is empty when nothing was released. Every successful item carries its own warnings array. An unknown tag fails that item only: tags are a closed dictionary, see tags_list and tags_create.";

pub const TASKS_CLOSE_MANY: &str = "Close several tasks in one call. Each item is applied independently and the response reports ok/error per item; a failing item does not cancel the others. Every task_id must use the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form. Returns: {items, succeeded, failed} - every item is {index, id, ok, task, changes, released, warnings} when it worked and {index, id, ok:false, error:{kind, message}} when it failed, where index is the position in the request list and id is the id of the task.";

pub const TASKS_DELETE: &str = "Soft-delete a task by task_id. The task_id must use the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form (e.g. 'LOG-82'). Deleting drops the blocker links of the task and detaches it from its epic, so the affected tasks change as well - they are listed in released. Returns: {id, deleted_at, changes, released}.";

pub const TASKS_RESTORE: &str = "Restore a soft-deleted task by task_id: clears deleted_at and the task is back on the board. Find deleted tasks with tasks_list(include_deleted: true). The task id must use the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form (e.g. 'LOG-82'). Deleting detaches a task from its epic and drops its blocker links \u{2014} restoring does not bring those back, set them again with tasks_update. Returns: the restored task - {id, project_id, number, title, description, status, priority, is_epic, epic_id, scope_id, source_file, tags, estimate, assignee, due_at, attachments_count, created_at, updated_at, closed_at, closed_reason, blocked_reason, deleted_at, project_name, scope_name, short_description, blocked_by, blocks, relates_to, duplicates, duplicated_by, children_counts}";

pub const TASKS_MOVE: &str = "Move a task to another project: project_id is the destination project. The task_id must use the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form (e.g. 'LOG-82'). Returns: the moved task - {id, project_id, number, title, description, status, priority, is_epic, epic_id, scope_id, source_file, tags, estimate, assignee, due_at, attachments_count, created_at, updated_at, closed_at, closed_reason, blocked_reason, deleted_at, project_name, scope_name, short_description, blocked_by, blocks, relates_to, duplicates, duplicated_by, children_counts}";

pub const TASKS_ADD_BLOCKER: &str = "Link two tasks: the task `task_id` becomes blocked by the task `blocked_by` (that is the blocker). Both ids use the full task id form 'PREFIX-NUMBER'. The blocker may live in another project. A task cannot block itself and cycles are rejected. The blocked task keeps its current status: set status 'blocked' with tasks_update yourself. The link is history and stays after the blocker is closed or the task is released, so never remove it for cleanup. Returns: the updated blocked task - {id, project_id, number, title, description, status, priority, is_epic, epic_id, scope_id, source_file, tags, estimate, assignee, due_at, attachments_count, created_at, updated_at, closed_at, closed_reason, blocked_reason, deleted_at, project_name, scope_name, short_description, blocked_by, blocks, relates_to, duplicates, duplicated_by, children_counts} with a changes array describing the link.";

pub const TASKS_ADD_LINK: &str = "Add a typed link between two tasks. link_type 'relates_to' marks the tasks as related (symmetric: visible from both, one record is stored); 'duplicates' marks `task_id` as a duplicate of `target` (directed). Both ids use the full task id form 'PREFIX-NUMBER'; the target may live in another project. A task cannot relate to or duplicate itself, duplicate cycles are rejected. Returns: the updated source task - {id, project_id, number, title, description, status, priority, is_epic, epic_id, scope_id, source_file, tags, estimate, assignee, due_at, attachments_count, created_at, updated_at, closed_at, closed_reason, blocked_reason, deleted_at, project_name, scope_name, short_description, blocked_by, blocks, relates_to, duplicates, duplicated_by, children_counts} with a changes array describing the link.";

pub const TASKS_REMOVE_BLOCKER: &str = "Remove a blocking link that was recorded by mistake: the task `task_id` is no longer blocked by the task `blocked_by`. Both ids use the full task id form 'PREFIX-NUMBER'. A closed blocker and a task that already left status 'blocked' are not reasons to remove the link: it is the history of the wait and must stay in blocked_by. If no open blocker remains, the blocked task leaves status 'blocked' and returns to the status it had before it was blocked ('open' when that status is unknown). Returns: the updated blocked task - {id, project_id, number, title, description, status, priority, is_epic, epic_id, scope_id, source_file, tags, estimate, assignee, due_at, attachments_count, created_at, updated_at, closed_at, closed_reason, blocked_reason, deleted_at, project_name, scope_name, short_description, blocked_by, blocks, relates_to, duplicates, duplicated_by, children_counts} with a changes array describing the link and the status release.";

pub const TASKS_REMOVE_LINK: &str = "Remove a relates_to or duplicates link between two tasks. Both ids must use the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form. If the link does not exist, returns not_found. Returns: the updated source task - {id, project_id, number, title, description, status, priority, is_epic, epic_id, scope_id, source_file, tags, estimate, assignee, due_at, attachments_count, created_at, updated_at, closed_at, closed_reason, blocked_reason, deleted_at, project_name, scope_name, short_description, blocked_by, blocks, relates_to, duplicates, duplicated_by, children_counts} with a changes array describing the removal.";
pub const TASKS_DELETED_LIST: &str = "List the soft-deleted tasks of the backlog: the ones that were deleted and are still recoverable. Returns: {items, total}, newest first. A deleted task keeps its id, number, title, description and tags, but it is missing from every other task list; restore it with tasks_restore. Supports the project_id and q filters and pagination via limit and offset.";
pub const TASKS_GET_MANY: &str = "Get multiple tasks by their IDs in a single call. Each id must use the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form (e.g. 'LOG-82'); a bare number is rejected because it is unique only inside a project. Returns: {items, missing} - items follow the requested order and carry the task fields, missing lists ids that do not exist, are soft-deleted or malformed. Use this instead of calling tasks_get in a loop.";
pub const TASKS_OPTIONS: &str = "List tasks as a compact picker source: used to fill the epic, blocker and related-task lists of the forms. Without q it returns every task of one project, ordered by number. With q it searches the whole backlog, so a task of another project can be found by its id or title. Returns: {items, total}. The description is left out of the items.";
pub const TASKS_REVISIONS_LIST: &str = "Read the previous texts of one task: the description and the comment bodies that were replaced by an edit. Read-only. Returns: {items, total}, oldest first; each item is { id, task_id, field, ref_id, body, created_at }, where field is 'description' or 'comment' and ref_id is the id of the edited comment for a comment revision. The current text is not included. Use it to see how a description changed; the activity log only names the touched fields.";
pub const TASKS_STATS: &str = "Read the aggregate task counts shown by the navigation and the filters: the number of non-epic tasks per status across every project, and every epic with children_counts. Read-only. Soft-deleted tasks are not counted. Returns: {tasks, epics} - tasks is a map of status to count, epics lists each epic as a task with children_counts: the number of its child tasks per status plus their total. Use it instead of counting a loaded page.";

pub const ACTIVITY_LIST: &str = "Read the interaction log of the whole backlog: every recorded change with its time, source ('ui', 'mcp' or 'cli'), client, operation, entity type and id, the list of touched fields and the changes with their old and new values. An attachment event carries the file name as the value and the attachment id as ref_id. client is the self-declared name/version of the MCP client that made the change, null when the client declared none or the change came from the UI. Supports filters (source, op, entity, from, to) and pagination via limit and offset. Returns: {items, total, ops}, where ops is the list of known operation names.";

pub const TASKS_HISTORY: &str = "Show the change log of one task: when it was changed, from which surface (source: 'ui' or 'mcp'), which MCP client did it (client: the name/version the client declared, null when it declared none) and which fields were touched. Returns: {items, total}, newest first; each item is { ts, source, client, op, fields, changes }. A changed field keeps the old and the new value, a creation lists only the fields set beyond the defaults; an attachment event carries the file name as the value and the attachment id as ref_id. Reads are not logged themselves. The history of a task follows it after a move to another project.";

pub const PROJECTS_LIST: &str = "List projects; set includeArchived to also return archived ones. q filters by a case-insensitive substring of the project name, id, prefix or repo. repo matches the repository exactly after normalization to host/owner/name. Every item carries task_counts and epic_counts: the number of its non-epic tasks and epics per status. Returns: {items} - every item is {id, name, prefix, repo, created_at, archived_at, task_counts, epic_counts} with task_counts and epic_counts.";

pub const PROJECTS_GET: &str = "Get a single project by project_id, including task counts by status: task_counts counts non-epic tasks, epic_counts counts epics. scope_counts lists the project's scopes with their task and epic totals. Returns: the project - {id, name, prefix, repo, created_at, archived_at, task_counts, epic_counts} with task_counts, epic_counts and scope_counts.";

pub const PROJECTS_CREATE: &str = "Create a project. name, project_id and prefix are required. The project_id is a lowercase slug of latin letters, digits and dashes; it is the key of the project in links and in the project_id parameter, and it cannot be changed later. The prefix is 1-6 uppercase letters or digits, it is stored uppercase and starts every task id of the project, and it cannot be changed later either. repo is optional and accepts a git URL; it is stored as host/owner/name. Returns: the created project - {id, name, prefix, repo, created_at, archived_at, task_counts, epic_counts}.";

pub const PROJECTS_UPDATE: &str = "Rename a project, change its archived flag or set its repository. Pass the fields to change next to project_id (e.g. {project_id: 'spa', name: 'Shop'}); a nested patch object is accepted too. The prefix is immutable \u{2014} it defines task ids. repo takes host/owner/name or a git URL and null clears it. Returns: the updated project - {id, name, prefix, repo, created_at, archived_at, task_counts, epic_counts}.";

pub const SCOPES_LIST: &str = "List scopes, each with task_count (active non-epic tasks assigned, i.e. open/in-progress/blocked), tasks_total (all unfinished and closed non-epic tasks assigned) and epics_count (non-deleted epics assigned). Pass project_id to limit to one project; omit it to list scopes across all projects. Returns: {items} - every item is {id, project_id, name, created_at, task_count, tasks_total, epics_count}.";

pub const SCOPES_GET: &str = "Get a single scope by scope_id, including its task_count (active non-epic tasks), tasks_total (all non-epic tasks) and epics_count. Returns: the scope - {id, project_id, name, created_at, task_count, tasks_total, epics_count}.";

pub const SCOPES_PROJECT_IDS: &str = "List ids of all projects that have at least one scope. Returns: {items} - the list of project ids.";

pub const SCOPES_CREATE: &str = "Create a scope in a project. A scope groups the tasks of that project; a task points at its scope through scope_id. Returns: the created scope - {id, project_id, name, created_at, task_count, tasks_total, epics_count}.";

pub const SCOPES_UPDATE: &str = "Rename a scope by scope_id: pass the new name next to scope_id (e.g. {scope_id: 3, name: 'Design'}); a nested patch object {scope_id, patch: {name}} is accepted too. Returns: the updated scope - {id, project_id, name, created_at, task_count, tasks_total, epics_count}.";

pub const SCOPES_DELETE: &str = "Delete a scope by scope_id; its tasks lose the scope assignment. Pass dry_run: true to preview the deletion - it returns the scope and the ids of the tasks and epics that would lose it, and changes nothing. Returns: {scope, task_ids, epic_ids, dry_run} - the deleted scope and the ids of the tasks and epics that lost it; with dry_run the same report without changing anything.";

pub const TAGS_LIST: &str = "Read the tags in use: every tag with the number of tasks that carry it and the number of open ones. Read-only. Use it before filtering or assigning a tag: the tag list is closed, so tasks_create and tasks_update reject a tag that is not in the dictionary. Archived tags are not listed and cannot be assigned. Accepts the filters project_id and status; soft-deleted tasks are not counted. Returns: {items, total}, where each item is { tag, count, active }, most used first. active counts the tasks that are open, in-progress or blocked: backlog and closed tasks are not counted.";

pub const TAGS_DICTIONARY: &str = "List every tag name in the dictionary, including the tags that no task carries yet. Archived tags are excluded: they cannot be assigned. Use tags_list for the counts of the tags in use. Returns: {items} - the tag names, sorted by name.";

pub const TAGS_INVENTORY: &str = "The whole tag dictionary including the archived tags and the tags no task carries, with usage counts. Counts soft-deleted tasks out. Archived tags stay on the tasks that already carry them but cannot be assigned again. `active` counts open, in-progress and blocked tasks, `backlog` counts the backlog ones. Returns: {items, total}, where each item is {name, archived, created_at, count, active, backlog}.";

pub const TAGS_CREATE: &str = "Add a tag to the dictionary. Tags are a closed list: tasks_create and tasks_update accept only names that are in it. Names are trimmed and lowercased. Calling it on an existing tag changes nothing, and an archived tag is restored. Returns: the tag - {name, archived, created_at}.";

pub const TAGS_UPDATE: &str = "Archive or restore a tag by name: pass archived next to name (e.g. {name: 'design', archived: true}); a nested patch object is accepted too. An archived tag keeps the tasks that already carry it, but it disappears from tags_list and cannot be assigned again. Returns: the tag - {name, archived, created_at}.";

pub const TAGS_RENAME: &str = "Rename a tag everywhere: the dictionary entry and every task that carries it. Fails when the new name is already taken. Returns: the tag - {name, archived, created_at}.";

pub const COMMENTS_LIST: &str = "List the comments of a task, oldest first. Deleted comments are hidden. The task id must use the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form (e.g. 'LOG-82'). Returns: {items, total} - every item is a comment - {id, task_id, body, created_at, edited_at, deleted_at}.";

pub const COMMENTS_ADD: &str = "Add a comment to a task. The task id must use the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form (e.g. 'LOG-82'). The body is Markdown and formatting is rendered: a short log or config in a fenced block is welcome, source code belongs in the repository. Returns: the created comment - {id, task_id, body, created_at, edited_at, deleted_at}. Editing a comment later keeps its previous text as a revision.";

pub const COMMENTS_EDIT: &str = "Edit the text of a comment by comment_id, the `id` field of an item from tasks_comments_list. The body is Markdown and formatting is rendered, as in tasks_comments_add. The previous text is kept as a revision, so nothing is lost. Returns: the updated comment - {id, task_id, body, created_at, edited_at, deleted_at} with edited_at set.";

pub const COMMENTS_DELETE: &str = "Soft-delete a comment by comment_id, the `id` field of an item from tasks_comments_list. The comment disappears from the task, but its id and history stay in the database. Returns: the deleted comment - {id, task_id, body, created_at, edited_at, deleted_at} with deleted_at set.";
pub const ATTACHMENTS_LIST: &str = "List the files attached to one task, without the ones deleted. Read-only. Returns: {items} - every item is {id, task_id, file_name, mime_type, size, created_at, uri, missing, deleted_at}. The uri of an item is an MCP resource holding the bytes of the file: read it with resources/read instead of looking for the file on disk. A file that is gone from the media folder is still listed, with missing set to true.";

pub const ATTACHMENTS_DELETED_LIST: &str = "List the soft-deleted attachments, newest deletion first. Read-only. Give task_id to list the deleted attachments of one task only, omit it to list every deleted attachment. Soft-deleted files are kept for 30 days, then the file and the row are removed for good. Returns: {items} - every item is {id, task_id, file_name, mime_type, size, created_at, uri, missing, deleted_at} with deleted_at set and task_title - the title of the task the file returns to.";

pub const ATTACHMENTS_ADD: &str = "Attach a file to a task. The file is stored in the media folder of the app, the task keeps only a reference to it. Use it for a raw log, a dump, a config or a dataset that the task needs and that is too big or too volatile for the text. Give either path (a file on the machine where the app runs) or content_base64 (the file content encoded as base64) together with filename. Returns: {item} - the created attachment - {id, task_id, file_name, mime_type, size, created_at, uri, missing, deleted_at}.";

pub const ATTACHMENTS_DELETE: &str = "Soft-delete one attachment of a task: the file stays in the media folder and the row keeps its deleted_at, so the attachment can be restored with attachments_restore for 30 days. It disappears from attachments_list and from the task right away. The attachment_id is the `id` field of an item from attachments_list. Returns: the deleted attachment - {id, task_id, file_name, mime_type, size, created_at, uri, missing, deleted_at} with deleted_at set.";

pub const ATTACHMENTS_RESTORE: &str = "Restore a soft-deleted attachment: it goes back to the task it was attached to, nothing is copied or moved. Find deleted attachments with attachments_deleted_list. Fails when the task is deleted (restore the task first) or when the file is gone from the media folder. Returns: the restored attachment - {id, task_id, file_name, mime_type, size, created_at, uri, missing, deleted_at} with deleted_at set to null.";

pub const ATTACHMENTS_MOVE: &str = "Move an attachment to another task. The attachment keeps its id, its uri and its history, only the task it belongs to changes, so links, ref_id values and resources keep working. A soft-deleted attachment can be moved too, and it lands on the target task restored, with deleted_at cleared: that is the way to bring a deleted file back onto a different task. The file is not copied, the bytes stay where they are, so the move works even when the file is already gone from the media folder. Fails when the target is the task the attachment is already on, when the target does not exist or is deleted, and when the attachment_id is unknown. Use attachments_copy when the file should stay on the source task as well. Returns: {item, previous_task_id, was_deleted} - item is the attachment on its new task, previous_task_id is the task it left, was_deleted tells whether it was soft-deleted before the move.";

pub const ATTACHMENTS_COPY: &str = "Copy an attachment to another task. A new, independent attachment is created from the same bytes: it has its own id and uri, its own row and its own file, so deleting, moving or purging one copy does not touch the other. The copy keeps file_name, mime_type and size of the source and gets the current time as created_at. The source attachment is not changed in any way. Use attachments_move when the file belongs to one task and only its task changes. Fails when the target is the task the attachment is already on, when the target does not exist or is deleted, and when the file is gone from the media folder. Returns: {item} - the created attachment - {id, task_id, file_name, mime_type, size, created_at, uri, missing, deleted_at}.";

pub const ATTACHMENTS_PURGE: &str = "Remove a soft-deleted attachment for good, now instead of waiting for the automatic cleanup: the file in the media folder and the row are gone and cannot be restored. Only works on a soft-deleted attachment. Returns: {id, task_id, file_name, file_removed} - file_removed is false when the file was already gone from the media folder.";

pub const ATTACHMENTS_VERIFY: &str = "Check the files of the attachments: which ones are still in the media folder and which ones are gone. Read-only, soft-deleted attachments are skipped. Returns: {ok, missing} - ok is the list of attachments still in the media folder, missing the ones that are gone. Give task_id to check one task only, omit it to check everything.";

pub const BACKUP_CREATE: &str = "Create a manual backup of the database. Writes a copy into the manual backup folder with a date-time stamp and returns its path. This tool only creates backups; it cannot restore them. Returns: {path} - the path of the written copy.";
pub const BACKUP_LIST: &str = "List the existing database backups: the auto snapshots taken before MCP writes, the daily and weekly copies and the manual ones. Returns: {items} newest first, each item is { kind, name, path, size, mtime }. Restoring is not available here: the user picks the snapshot on the Backups page of the UI, so name the file they should restore.";
pub const BACKUPS_INSPECT: &str = "Check a backup before restoring it: the header, the integrity check, the migrations it carries and the number of tasks and projects inside. Not exposed to MCP.";
pub const BACKUPS_RESTORE: &str = "Replace the whole database with the chosen backup. The current state is copied into the manual folder first and every open tab is reloaded afterwards. Not exposed to MCP.";

pub const REQUEST_LOG_LIST: &str = "Read the log of MCP tool calls, kept for analysing how agents use the tracker. Every call is recorded, including reads, validation failures and failed operations. Long text fields (description, comment body, attachment content_base64) are replaced with <omitted>, other parameters are kept. Filter by op, status, client, params_hash and the from/to period; paginate with limit (default 50, max 200) and offset. params_hash is stable for the same tool name and parameters, so group by it to find repeated calls. Returns: {items, total}, newest first; each item is {ts, client, op, params_hash, params, status, error_kind, error_message, duration_ms}. The log is kept 30 days by default (XBOCT_LOG_REQUEST_LOG_KEEP_DAYS).";

pub const REQUEST_LOG_EXPORT: &str = "Export the MCP tool-call log as newline-delimited JSON, one call per line, oldest first. Accepts the same filters as request_log_list; limit defaults to 10000 (max 10000) and offset skips rows. Long text fields are omitted the same way. Returns: {content, count} - content is the JSONL text, count is the number of lines.";

pub const PLANS_LIST: &str = "List all plans across every project: a plan has no project_id and its items may come from any project. Each item carries tasks_count and epics_count (soft-deleted tasks are not counted). Returns: {items} - sorted by name; use plans_get for one plan's items.";
pub const PLANS_GET: &str = "Get one plan by plan_id with tasks_count and epics_count. Returns: the plan - {id, name, description, status, created_at, updated_at, closed_at, deleted_at, tasks_count, epics_count}.";
pub const PLANS_CREATE: &str = "Create a global plan. name is required; description defaults to an empty string and status to 'backlog' ('backlog', 'active', 'done', 'cancelled'). Names need not be unique: several imported markdown files may share one. Returns: the created plan.";
pub const PLANS_UPDATE: &str = "Patch a plan's name, description or status by plan_id; pass fields next to plan_id or in a nested patch object. status is manual and never derived from the tasks in the plan. Pass expected_updated_at (the current updated_at) to reject a stale edit with a conflict. Returns: the updated plan.";
pub const PLANS_DELETE: &str = "Soft-delete a plan by plan_id. Its items, positions, notes and document links are kept, so plans_restore brings the whole plan back. Returns: the deleted plan with deleted_at set.";
pub const PLANS_RESTORE: &str = "Restore a soft-deleted plan by plan_id with all its items, positions, notes and document links. Find deleted plans with plans_deleted_list. Returns: the restored plan with deleted_at cleared.";
pub const PLANS_DELETED_LIST: &str =
    "List the soft-deleted plans, newest deletion first. Read-only. Returns: {items}.";
pub const PLANS_ITEMS_ADD: &str = "Add a task or an epic to a plan by its full task id (e.g. 'LOG-42'); the task may belong to any project and an epic can be an item too, without pulling in its children. The plan stores the task's internal id, so a later tasks_move leaves the item, its position and its notes untouched and only the displayed key changes. Adding the same task twice is a conflict. Returns: the added item - {key, title, project_id, is_epic, position, deleted}.";
pub const PLANS_ITEMS_REMOVE: &str = "Remove a task or epic from a plan by plan_id and task_id. Notes attached to that task stay in the plan and return if the item is added again. Returns: the removed item, or null when the plan had no such item.";
pub const PLANS_REORDER: &str = "Set the order of a plan's items: task_ids must list every current item exactly once, in the new order; tasks and epics from different projects share one sequence. Rejects a key that no longer resolves after a task move, an incomplete list or a duplicate, and changes nothing in that case. Idempotent. Returns: {items} - the reordered items with positions in steps of 1024.";
pub const PLANS_NOTES_LIST: &str = "List the notes of one plan, oldest first; deleted notes are hidden. Returns: {items} - each note is {id, plan_id, key, body, created_at, edited_at, deleted_at}, where key is the current id of the task it belongs to.";
pub const PLANS_NOTES_ADD: &str = "Add a Markdown note to an item of a plan. The task must already be an item of that plan; its id uses the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form. Returns: the created note.";
pub const PLANS_NOTES_EDIT: &str = "Replace the body of a plan note by note_id. The previous text is kept in text_revisions. Returns: the updated note with edited_at set.";
pub const PLANS_NOTES_DELETE: &str = "Soft-delete a plan note by note_id: it disappears from plans_notes_list but keeps its id and history. Returns: the note with deleted_at set.";
pub const DOCUMENTS_LIST: &str = "List working documents (ADRs, PRDs, architecture, research, workflow notes, guides, specs) across every project; pass project_id to narrow to one project. Soft-deleted documents are hidden. Each document has its own 'PREFIX-NNN' key in its project. Returns: {items}.";
pub const DOCUMENTS_GET: &str = "Get one document by document_id: either the internal id or its project key 'PREFIX-NNN'. Keys are case-insensitive and may be trimmed; bare numbers are rejected. Returns: {id, project_id, number, key, kind, title, body, status, source_file, created_at, updated_at, closed_at, deleted_at}.";
pub const DOCUMENTS_CREATE: &str = "Create a document in a project with a kind (adr, prd, architecture, research, workflow, guide, spec), title, Markdown body and optional source_file. The key is allocated in the project's prefix sequence and never changes. Returns: the created document.";
pub const DOCUMENTS_UPDATE: &str = "Patch a document's kind, title, body, status (draft, accepted, superseded, archived) or source_file by document_id (internal id or 'PREFIX-NNN' key); pass fields next to document_id or in a nested patch object. The previous body is stored in text_revisions. Pass expected_updated_at (the current updated_at) to reject a stale edit with a conflict. Returns: the updated document.";
pub const DOCUMENTS_DELETE: &str = "Soft-delete a document by document_id (internal id or 'PREFIX-NNN' key). Its links to plans and revisions are kept, so documents_restore brings it back. Returns: the deleted document with deleted_at set.";
pub const DOCUMENTS_RESTORE: &str = "Restore a soft-deleted document by document_id (internal id or 'PREFIX-NNN' key) with its links and revisions. Find deleted documents with documents_deleted_list. Returns: the restored document with deleted_at cleared.";
pub const DOCUMENTS_DELETED_LIST: &str =
    "List soft-deleted documents newest first. Returns: {items}.";
pub const DOCUMENTS_REVISIONS_LIST: &str = "List the earlier body texts of a document by document_id (internal id or 'PREFIX-NNN' key), newest first, from text_revisions(entity='document'). Returns: {items} - each revision is {id, entity, entity_id, field, body, created_at}.";
pub const PLANS_DOCUMENTS_LIST: &str = "List the documents linked to a plan by plan_id; a plan is global and may link documents of any project. Returns: {items} - full documents.";
pub const PLANS_DOCUMENTS_LINK: &str = "Link a document to a plan by plan_id and document_id (internal id or 'PREFIX-NNN' key). The document may belong to any project; no project match is required. Repeated links are ignored. Returns: the linked document.";
pub const PLANS_DOCUMENTS_UNLINK: &str = "Remove a document link from a plan by plan_id and document_id (internal id or 'PREFIX-NNN' key); the document itself is not deleted or changed. Returns: the unlinked document.";
